1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,863
Demo videos drift from what they promise.

2
00:00:02,983 --> 00:00:05,410
A step gets cut, a line changes,

3
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:07,489
and the checklist still says done.

4
00:00:07,609 --> 00:00:10,823
SLEUTH connects a demo's script and checklist to evidence,

5
00:00:10,823 --> 00:00:13,680
so you can see what needs another look.

6
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:16,580
Then decide what still needs a human review.

7
00:00:18,050 --> 00:00:20,040
Here's the public ProofPay demo.

8
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:23,312
Its sample results were prepared ahead of time;

9
00:00:23,312 --> 00:00:26,070
no analysis is running in this walkthrough.

10
00:00:26,190 --> 00:00:30,178
The claims map puts each checklist item beside its evidence.

11
00:00:30,298 --> 00:00:34,070
Open a marker, and the Evidence Lantern explains what was

12
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:37,465
found, what wasn't, and the limits of the check.

13
00:00:37,585 --> 00:00:40,313
That confidence label helps rank a match.

14
00:00:40,433 --> 00:00:41,684
It isn't proof.

15
00:00:41,804 --> 00:00:45,673
That makes the uncertainty visible before anyone signs off.

16
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:49,105
Let's take a closer look.

17
00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:52,062
The Lantern separates what was found from what

18
00:00:52,062 --> 00:00:54,899
wasn't, then explains the limits of the match.

19
00:00:55,019 --> 00:00:58,769
Spoken words and sampled frames can suggest evidence, but

20
00:00:58,769 --> 00:01:02,102
they don't establish that a step was completed.

21
00:01:02,222 --> 00:01:05,376
That's why the next action is a person's decision.

22
00:01:07,890 --> 00:01:12,657
These are sample results, so the review actions demonstrate the workflow.

23
00:01:12,777 --> 00:01:15,090
To try it ourselves, we make a

24
00:01:15,090 --> 00:01:17,072
guest copy of the public demo.

25
00:01:17,692 --> 00:01:20,181
Here, we confirm the first three lines.

26
00:01:20,301 --> 00:01:23,992
The encryption claim needs outside proof: a video

27
00:01:23,992 --> 00:01:27,682
alone can't establish how customer data is protected.

28
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:32,815
The report brings those decisions together.

29
00:01:32,935 --> 00:01:36,338
The PDF export has no usable evidence.

30
00:01:36,458 --> 00:01:39,679
That's a Ghost, so there's no candidate to confirm.

31
00:01:39,799 --> 00:01:43,312
Instead, we ask for a reshoot and explain what to film.

32
00:01:43,432 --> 00:01:46,786
With the blockers addressed, we apply the Final Seal.

33
00:01:46,906 --> 00:01:50,554
The note makes clear this is a walkthrough on sample data.

34
00:01:52,192 --> 00:01:54,282
Notice what signing off means here.

35
00:01:54,402 --> 00:01:57,930
A reshoot request doesn't create missing evidence,

36
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:01,458
and an outside-proof label doesn't verify encryption.

37
00:02:01,578 --> 00:02:03,493
Those limits stay in the report.

38
00:02:03,613 --> 00:02:07,072
The seal records a decision on a specific version.

39
00:02:07,192 --> 00:02:09,661
It isn't an accuracy score, or a

40
00:02:09,661 --> 00:02:11,778
guarantee that every claim is true.

41
00:02:12,645 --> 00:02:15,466
Now watch what happens when the script changes.

42
00:02:15,586 --> 00:02:19,022
We reword one sentence, and its evidence goes stale.

43
00:02:19,142 --> 00:02:23,043
That's a Poltergeist: a reminder to check that line again.

44
00:02:23,163 --> 00:02:25,263
The other lines keep their evidence.

45
00:02:25,383 --> 00:02:28,184
Back on the report, the seal is

46
00:02:28,184 --> 00:02:30,986
broken because the evidence changed after sign-off.

47
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:36,781
Built with Kiro: specs, steering, hooks, and builder agents.

48
00:02:37,549 --> 00:02:39,649
SLEUTH won't catch every problem.

49
00:02:39,769 --> 00:02:42,182
It makes the evidence and its limits

50
00:02:42,182 --> 00:02:44,596
visible, while a person makes the decision.

51
00:02:44,716 --> 00:02:48,129
This walkthrough uses an AI narration voice.
